  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 341 AM EDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nGOES water vapor imagery this early morning shows a stream of \nPacific monsoonal moisture trailing across the UP as a closed 500mb \nlow enters into the province of Ontario. With most stationary land \nand marine observation platforms showing near dead calm and dewpoint \ndepressions of 0 degrees, fog is pervasive across the CWA tonight. \nAll TAF sites currently show visibilities persistent at 1/4 mi or \nless and ceilings at or below 200 feet, and METAR sites are \nreporting fog or dense fog. With widespread fog currently evolving, \nhave hoisted a Dense Fog Advisory for most of the CWA.\n\nHigh surface moisture should be alleviated into the morning hours as \nthe Ontario low tracks east, moving subgeostrophic flow and \nsubsidence over the area. The tighter pressure gradient associated \nwith the center of the low comes into closer proximity, causing \nwinds to increase as diurnal heating kicks in. All this to say, fog \nis not expected to linger long into the morning. In fact, high \ntemperatures well into the 80s and resultant deep mixing with a dry \nairmass aloft will lead to RHs dropping down into the high 20-\npercents over the west this afternoon (40s/50s in the east). The NBM \nshows over 90% probability of highs above 85F for much of the \nwestern and southern UP, making this the warmest day the UP has had \nin a while. Given gusts to 20 mph in the western and central UP \n(higher in the Keweenaw), spots of borderline elevated fire \nconditions may occur for a short while this afternoon.\n\nAs the Ontario low track east toward Quebec throughout the day \ntoday, a weak front swings down from the northwest. As of now, a few \nof the CAMs are suggesting isolated rain/thunder cells between 22Z-\n00Z. Soundings are showing elevated CAPE above a small inversion \nduring the evening hours and a far from saturated atmosphere. Thus, \nopted not to include this possibility in current PoP issuance.\n\nWeak warm frontogenesis will occur Tuesday night/Wednesday morning \nto our south as PWATs increase again to 1.25-1.50 inches, with \nguidance mixed on whether associated rainfall will reach far enough \nnorth into the UP. Best chances (up to 40%) over the south. \n\nFor late week into the Labor Day weekend, ridging builds over the \nnorthern Plains, while a deep trough evolves over the Canadian \nMaritimes. While this pattern is generally favorable for subsidence \nover the local area, as evidenced by a high pressure ridge building \nin from the northeast, western parts of the UP may be close enough \nto the moisture/instability reservoir to see convection at times. \nCurrently capping out at 20-30% PoPs though, so the evolution is far \nfrom certain.",
